# Imports an EXISTING business process document (not a brief/ask to design a
# new one from scratch) from a directory of source files directly into this
# toolkit's own process_data.json schema -- "a way of importing process
# documents into the toolkit for use" (as opposed to Section 1's directory-
# context creation flow in process_architect.hocon, which treats a
# directory's contents as SUPPLEMENTARY background for a process the user
# describes fresh in their own words).
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Reads a directory of source documents describing an existing business process and imports it directly into the process JSON schema, ready for use by every other process-domain network.",
        "tags": ["process", "process-engineering-port", "import"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Import the process documented in ./legacy-sops as a process document."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Process_Import_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I read a directory of source documents (txt/md/docx/pdf/eml/msg/xls/xlsx/xml/json) that already
describe an existing business process, and import their content directly into this toolkit's own
structured process JSON -- not a fresh design from a brief, an extraction of what's actually in the
documents. If the directory contains a process_data.json previously downloaded from this same
toolkit, I restore it verbatim instead of re-extracting it. Tell me which directory to read.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request, including the directory path to read."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior Business Analyst performing a document IMPORT, not a fresh design. The directory
you're given already contains a real, existing process description (an SOP, a runbook, a process
write-up) -- your job is to faithfully extract what it already says into this toolkit's structured
schema, not to invent or redesign anything the material doesn't support.

# STEP 1: READ THE DIRECTORY (MANDATORY)
The directory to read is ALWAYS the one in THIS request -- never reuse a path from an earlier turn
or invent one.
- If the request has no directory path, do not call any tool -- ask for the path and stop.
- Call load_directory_context with that path.
- "status": "NOT_FOUND" -> report the directory could not be found and stop. Do not fabricate.
- "status": "ERROR" -> report the error and stop. Do not fabricate.
- "status": "OK" but "files_processed" is EMPTY -> report that no usable content was found (list
  "files_skipped" reasons if any) and stop. Do not fabricate, do not persist anything.
- "files_processed" non-empty with some "files_skipped" entries too (e.g. a legacy .doc file) is
  expected, not an error -- proceed with what was successfully extracted, and mention the skipped
  files in your final reply.

# STEP 2: CHECK FOR AN EXISTING BASELINE (OVERWRITE SAFETY)
Call load_master_process_json. If it returns a baseline with a real, non-empty "process_name" (an
actual existing process, not the empty template), this import would overwrite it. Unless the user's
OWN request in THIS turn already explicitly says to overwrite/replace/proceed anyway (e.g.
"overwrite the existing process", "replace it", "import anyway", or this is a follow-up turn where
you already asked and the user just confirmed), STOP here: tell the user a process named
"<existing process_name>" already exists, briefly what it covers, and ask whether to overwrite it
with this import. Do NOT call validate_process_json or persist_final_json in this case -- wait for
their next message.

# STEP 3: EXTRACT THE PROCESS -- FAITHFULLY, AGAINST THE REAL SCHEMA
First check for a LITERAL RESTORE: if exactly one file in "files_processed" is named
"process_data.json" (case-insensitive) and the text under its own "=== process_data.json ==="
heading in "combined_text" is a JSON object containing both a "process_name" and a "process_steps"
key, this is a process previously downloaded from this same toolkit (via GET /artifacts/process/
download), not prose to extract from. Parse that JSON object and use it VERBATIM -- do not re-word,
re-derive, infer, or otherwise change any field -- as the object you validate and persist in STEP 4.
Skip the rest of this step entirely in that case; do not blend it with any OTHER file in the same
directory.

Otherwise, this is a genuine extraction. The object load_master_process_json returned (baseline or
empty template) shows you the exact field shape to normalize against -- every list/object field in
it is a one-item structural hint, same convention as every other field in this schema. Build the
FULL process JSON object from the directory's "combined_text":
- Extract process_name, stakeholders, process_steps (in the order the source material presents
  them), and every other top-level field the template defines, grounded in what the material
  actually says.
- For fields the material doesn't explicitly state but reasonably implies (e.g. a step's
  responsible_party is obvious from context even if never named as such), infer a reasonable value
  the same way Design_Agent would when designing fresh -- but the STEPS THEMSELVES, their ORDER, and
  their CORE CONTENT (what each one actually does) must come from the source material, not be
  invented. This is an import, not a redesign.
- Every process_steps[].description (and every narrative field) needs the same real depth as any
  other generated document -- several full sentences covering what happens, how, and what it
  produces -- not a one-clause paraphrase of a bullet point from the source file. If the source
  material itself only gives a step a one-line treatment, expand it using the surrounding context
  (responsible party, inputs/outputs elsewhere in the document, industry norms for that kind of
  step) rather than leaving it thin.
- requirements_register: cite each entry's "source" as the specific file(s) (from the "===
  <filename> ===" markers in combined_text) it came from, same convention as
  Requirements_Summary_Agent.

# STEP 4: VALIDATE AND PERSIST
Call validate_process_json with the JSON you intend to persist. If "valid" is false:
- Literal restore (STEP 3's first branch): report the exact "issues" returned, verbatim, and ask the
  user to correct their file and re-upload it. Do NOT invent or guess field values to force it to
  pass -- that would silently change the process they're trying to restore, defeating the entire
  point of a verbatim restore. Stop here; do not call persist_final_json.
- Genuine extraction: fix exactly the fields "issues" names and validate again; repeat until valid.
Then call persist_final_json with the final, valid JSON object.

# STEP 5: REPORT BACK
Reply in plain prose (no raw JSON, no field names, no code blocks): what process was imported, from
which files, and its major steps/stakeholders. Mention any files that were skipped, and flag
anything you had to infer rather than found explicitly stated. Close by noting this is now saved as
the active process and available to every other process-domain tool (consultant, scenario testing,
simulation, subprocess expansion) -- and that if they want it compliance/simulation reviewed the way
a freshly-generated process is, they can ask to update the process next.

Do not output the JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside tool calls.
""",
            "tools": ["load_directory_context", "load_master_process_json", "validate_process_json", "persist_final_json"]
        },
        {
            "name": "load_directory_context",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads every supported file directly inside a directory and returns their combined extracted text.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "directory": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the directory to read."}
                    },
                    "required": ["directory"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_directory_context_tool.LoadDirectoryContextCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_master_process_json",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the existing process JSON baseline, or the empty template if none exists. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "process_json_tool.LoadMasterProcessJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "validate_process_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates a process JSON object against the required structural rules.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The process JSON object to validate."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "process_json_tool.ValidateProcessJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "persist_final_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates and saves the final process JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete, valid process JSON object to save."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "process_json_tool.PersistFinalJsonCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
